{"kind":"request","text":"claude: ruling on the three options in your assert 1f6ac039. Take option one - the fold-side dated active cause. I am lifting the no-widening restriction I put on 8fcb63e4.\n\nWHY, PLAINLY. I wrote that restriction before either of us knew the projection was insufficient, and I wrote the escape hatch into the same paragraph for exactly this case. You used it correctly. Holding you to a scope decision I made in ignorance would be the wrong kind of consistency.\n\nThe deciding argument is codex simplification conclusion, and the evidence for it is your own lane. Your walk is a second copy of the fold staleness rule, and it drifted on its first outing, in the direction of a false allow on a gate that authorizes an irreversible merge and a cross-author retirement plan. A duplicated rule that drifts immediately will drift again. Option two would ship that copy knowingly and then delete it later, so its work is partly throwaway and it carries a drift-prone inference on an authority gate in the meantime. Option three abandons a defect we have both now confirmed is real.\n\nON OPTION TWO BEING SAFE. It is - fail-closed, a strict subset of today refusals, no new false allows - and if the fold change turns out to be much larger than it looks, say so and I will take option two as an interim with the traversal fix alone. I am not making that the plan, only naming the fallback so you are not stuck if the contract change balloons.\n\nWHAT I CARE ABOUT MOST IN THE CONDITIONS. Delete your walk. The whole point of this ruling is one authoritative fact instead of two inferences, and a head that adds the fold fact while leaving the cmd/gs walk in place would give us the drift problem plus a new field. Second, the restored-history case - a supersession that is itself superseded - needs a test, because that is precisely the case the projection could not express and the reason we are here.\n\nCREDIT WHERE IT IS DUE. You told me last time that no widening was needed, and this time you came back and said that was right about sequence and wrong about sufficiency, unprompted, with the offending line named in your own source. That is the behaviour that makes a report worth ratifying. Codex found the chain; you did not argue with it.\n\nHead 08552c03 does not merge, as you said. This child request is where the successor lands.","body":{"conditions":"Child of 8fcb63e4, taking the widening that request scoped out. The fold publishes one authoritative dated active-cause fact: for a record that describes a superseded world, the sequence of each retirement cause that is STILL ACTIVE, so a supersession that has itself been superseded is not counted. cmd/gs consumes that fact and its own worldMovedAfter walk is DELETED, not left beside it; no second copy of the staleness rule survives this head. docs/reference/architecture.md updated in the same head with its candidate artifact, since this changes a layer contract. The overstating sentence in docs/reference/gs/merge.md corrected in the same head. Codex TestReviewWorldMovedAfterDoesNotHideOlderNestedRetirement passes by name. A test for the case the projection could not express: a supersession that is itself superseded must NOT count as an active cause, so the merge is admitted. A test that a cause active at or before the verdict still refuses. Mutation checks: with the production change reverted and the tests kept, each of those three fails by name. If the ProfileVersion bumps, the artifact states the deployment order explicitly - resident and adapters restarted on the new binary before gs is rebuilt - because an old fold refuses new records and we have been bitten by that exact ordering before. Full gate set green at the exact head, artifacts resting on the promise, and the review request goes to codex.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}